6. EXISTING DATA AND ACCOUNT MIGRATION

Treat existing task data as valuable user data.

Never assume that all pre-existing local tasks belong to the currently authenticated user.

6.1 Before migration

Determine:

- Whether existing tasks are anonymous local data.
- Whether an existing account or user identifier is available.
- Whether sample tasks are distinguishable from real tasks.
- Whether multiple users may have used the same device.
- Whether existing completion remarks and recurrence data can be preserved.
- Whether duplicate records already exist.

6.2 Safe migration

Implement a migration plan only after the ownership of existing records is understood.

Requirements:

- Back up data when feasible.
- Preserve task IDs and relationships.
- Preserve due dates, time zones, recurrence rules, timestamps, and completion history.
- Preserve completion remarks.
- Use database transactions or equivalent atomic operations where supported.
- Make migrations idempotent.
- Record migration status and failures.
- Prevent duplicate migration on repeated attempts.
- Provide a recovery strategy for interrupted migrations.

If anonymous tasks need to be associated with an account, request explicit user confirmation before transferring them.

If ownership is uncertain, do not silently assign the records to an account.

Never delete, overwrite, merge, or upload existing local tasks without a documented migration strategy and the required user consent.

6.3 Sample data

Remove any unsafe behavior that automatically restores demo tasks when a database is empty.

Sample data must never overwrite, replace, or reappear over genuine user data.

If demo data is retained, isolate it behind an explicit demo or development mode.

